justify --> positions along the primary axis
align --> position along the cross asix
content --> a group that can be distributed
items --> single items that can be positioned individually

justify-content --> control distribution of the group along the primary axis
align-items --> position each item indiviually along the cross axis
algin-content --> position group of items along cross axis
(align-content used with flex-wrap mentioned below)

align-self --> command to align the element the style code is contained within
***there is no "justify-self" since only a group can move along the primary axis


flex-direction --> row or column, defaults to row

flex-basis --> sets size across primary axis (think width for row and height for column)

flex-grow --> sets behavior to fill empty space (if multiple children set flex-grow: 1; then the extra space is divided proportionally between them based on their flex-grow value)
^^^how empty space is distributed when items are smaller than container

flex-basis sets 'hypothetical size' of an element, and when the items shrink they will do so proportionally

flex-shrink --> sets how shrinking behavior is split between elements by a ratio, when you don't want them to shrink proportionally
^^^how space is removed from items when they are bigger than container
If you don't want something to shrink, you can set that with flex-shrink: 0;

flex-shrink won't shrink an element(item) below it's defaul min-width value.
	ex. a text input box on a website
This means if the container shrink enough the item will just overflow outside the container. This can be fixed by explicitly setting the min-width of an item to 0px, to force allow flex-shrink to shrink it.
(same thing can be done with min-height when using flexbox columns)

gap --> set amount of space in-between each child

auto-margins --> distribute the empty space to the elements margin
	so if margin-left: auto; --> the extra space on the page will be applied to the left margin of the item
	if multiple margins are set to auto on the same axis it will split the space between them


flex-wrap --> wraps the items onto the next row when the container shrinks, doesn't shrink items until they all exist in their own row



